.Dd November 5, 2026
.Dt CMC 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cmc
.Nd explicit-state model checker for C protocol code
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl -model-file Ar file
.Op Fl -binary Ar path
.Op Fl -bfs | Fl -simulate
.Op Fl -seed Ar n
.Op Fl -walks Ar k
.Op Fl -depth Ar d
.Op Fl -max-states Ar n
.Op Fl -time Ar seconds
.Op Fl -crashes Ar n
.Op Fl -drops Ar n
.Op Fl -timeouts Ar n
.Nm
.Fl -spec Ar file
.Op Fl -define Ar tag ...
.Nm
.Fl -emit-tla Ar file
.Op Fl -define Ar tag ...
.Nm
.Fl -refine Ar spec
.Fl -bind Ar file
.Fl -binary Ar path
.Op Fl -model-file Ar file
.Op Fl -simulate
.Nm
.Fl -model Ar name
.Op Fl -tlc-cfg Ar file | Fl -cfg Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value Ns Op , Ns ...
.Op Fl -inv Ar name Ns Op , Ns ...
.Op Fl -prop Ar name Ns Op , Ns ...
.Op Fl -all-props
.Op Fl -bfs | Fl -dfs Ar depth | Fl -simulate
.Op Fl -seed Ar n
.Op Fl -walks Ar k
.Op Fl -depth Ar d
.Op Fl -det
.Op Fl -max-states Ar n
.Op Fl -time Ar seconds
.Op Fl -trace-out Ar file
.Op Fl -progress Ar seconds
.Op Fl -quiet
.Op Fl -coverage
.Op Fl -clients Ar n
.Op Fl -drops Ar n
.Op Fl -dups Ar n
.Op Fl -crashes Ar n
.Op Fl -net-cap Ar n
.Op Fl -persist-cap Ar n
.Op Fl -reply-cap Ar n
.Op Fl -net Cm fifo | bag
.Op Fl -live-steps Ar k
.Nm
.Fl -replay Ar file
.Op Fl -inv Ar names | Fl -all-props
.Nm
.Fl -model Ar name
.Fl -list
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
explores every interleaving of a set of protocol nodes, the network
between them, their timeouts and their crashes, up to the budgets given,
and checks the properties in every state it reaches.
.Pp
With
.Fl -model-file ,
the nodes are the program you ship, run unmodified as processes; the
model file names the program, the environment to explore and the
invariants.
That is the way to check a product; see
.Sx Checking a program
below,
.Lk https://gregburd.codeberg.page/libcmc/reference/model-file/ "the model file" ,
and
.Xr cmc 7 .
.Pp
With
.Fl -model ,
the nodes are an in-process model: C step functions written against
.Xr libcmc 3 .
That interface is the engine's calibration path: the
.Cm hc
core reproduces TLC's results on a published consensus specification.
The rest of this page, from
.Sx Choosing the model and properties ,
describes it.
.Pp
.Nm make install
installs
.Nm .
A program that embeds the engine with in-process models of its own
writes a
.Fn main
that passes its table of models to
.Fn cmc_main ,
and links against
.Pa libcmc.a ;
that program is a
.Nm
too.
The binary built by this repository carries the in-process models
.Cm hc
(the calibration core),
.Cm toy ,
and
.Cm toy-bug1
to
.Cm toy-bug3 .
.Pp
Output follows the wording of TLC, the TLA+ model checker, so the two can
be compared line by line.
Depth counts the initial state as 1.
.Ss Checking a program
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -model-file Ar file
Check the program the model file names, with its environment and
invariants.
Linux x86-64 only: the nodes run under seccomp user notification.
Every key the file sets must be one
.Nm
implements; an unknown or unsupported key is an error, not ignored.
.It Fl -binary Ar path
Check this program instead of the file's
.Cm binary ,
for example a build with a planted bug.
It must be a non-PIE executable
.Pq built with Fl no-pie .
.It Fl -spec Ar file
Model-check an abstract specification
.Pq a Pa .cml No module
on its own, the way TLC checks a TLA+ module: no implementation, just
the declared variables, actions and invariants.
See
.Lk https://gregburd.codeberg.page/libcmc/reference/plan-tlaplus-scope/ "the TLA+-scope plan" .
.It Fl -emit-tla Ar file
Translate the abstract specification
.Pq a Pa .cml No module
to TLA+ on standard output, so it can be checked with TLC or Apalache
and proved with TLAPS.
Name the output after the module
.Pq Pa NAME.tla
so TLC accepts it.
.It Fl -define Ar tag
Activate an action gated with
.Op Ar tag
in a
.Pa .cml
spec; repeatable.
Used to turn on a planted-bug action.
.It Fl -refine Ar spec
Check that the implementation named by
.Fl -binary
or the model file
.Em refines
the abstract
.Ar spec
.Pq a Pa .cml No module .
At each reached implementation state the abstract invariants are
checked against the mapped state, so one abstract model validates many
implementations.
Requires
.Fl -bind .
.It Fl -bind Ar file
The binding
.Pq a Pa .bind No file
that maps the implementation's observables onto the abstract variables,
for
.Fl -refine .
.It Fl -crashes Ar n , Fl -drops Ar n , Fl -timeouts Ar n
Override the file's budgets.
.It Fl -depth Ar d
Bound the search to
.Ar d
actions; a search that reaches it is reported as incomplete.
With
.Fl -simulate ,
the length of each walk, default 100.
.El
.Pp
.Fl -bfs ,
.Fl -simulate ,
.Fl -seed ,
.Fl -walks ,
.Fl -max-states
and
.Fl -time
work as for in-process models, except that walk
.Va i
of a simulation does not take its own seed: the same
.Fl -seed
takes the same walks.
Any other option with
.Fl -model-file
is an error.
.Ss Choosing the model and properties
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -model Ar name
The model to check, by its name in the program's model table.
Required, except with
.Fl -replay .
.It Fl -cfg Ar key Ns = Ns Ar value Ns Op , Ns ...
Core settings, passed to the core's
.Fn cfg_set .
May be given more than once; later settings override earlier ones,
including those read by
.Fl -tlc-cfg .
.It Fl -tlc-cfg Ar file
Read a TLC
.Pa .cfg
file: its
.Sy CONSTANTS
become core settings, and its
.Sy INVARIANTS
and
.Sy PROPERTIES
become the properties to check.
.It Fl -inv Ar name Ns Op , Ns ... , Fl -prop Ar name Ns Op , Ns ...
Check these properties.
The two options are synonyms; each property's kind comes from the model.
.It Fl -all-props
Check every property of the model.
.It Fl -list
Print the model's property names and exit.
.It Fl -version
Print the libcmc version and exit.
.El
.Pp
A model may mark its first properties as always checked.
At least one property must be named, or
.Fl -all-props
given.
.Ss Choosing the search
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -bfs
Breadth-first search, the default.
It finds a shortest counterexample, and its state counts are exact.
.It Fl -dfs Ar depth
Depth-first search to
.Ar depth
states.
A search that reaches the bound without finishing is reported as
incomplete, never as a pass.
.It Fl -simulate
Seeded random walks, not exhaustive.
Walk
.Va i
uses seed
.Ar n Ns + Ns Va i ;
a violation is reported with the
.Fl -seed
and
.Fl -walks Cm 1
that reproduce it.
.It Fl -seed Ar n
First seed for
.Fl -simulate .
Default 0.
.It Fl -walks Ar k
Number of walks for
.Fl -simulate .
Default 1000.
.It Fl -depth Ar d
Length of each walk, in states.
Default 100.
.It Fl -replay Ar file
Run a replay file written by
.Fl -trace-out
through the real cores, printing each state.
The model, settings and budgets are taken from the file; an option on
the command line that conflicts with the file is refused.
.It Fl -det
Determinism gate: run every step twice from copies of the same state and
fail if the two runs differ in their outputs or their next state.
.It Fl -max-states Ar n
Stop after
.Ar n
distinct states.
.It Fl -time Ar seconds
Stop after
.Ar seconds
of wall time.
.El
.Ss Choosing the environment
These options apply to message-passing cores (actor mode).
Each budget is a count over the whole run, from 0 to 255.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -clients Ar n
Client requests each node may receive.
Default 1.
.It Fl -drops Ar n
Messages the network may lose.
With
.Fl -net Cm fifo ,
a drop resets the connection and both ends are told.
Default 0.
.It Fl -dups Ar n
Messages the network may duplicate.
Needs
.Fl -net Cm bag .
Default 0.
.It Fl -crashes Ar n
Crashes, of any node.
A crash keeps any prefix of the node's pending disk writes, loses the
rest and the volatile state, resets its connections and restarts it.
Default 0.
.It Fl -net-cap Ar n
Messages in flight at once.
Default 8.
.It Fl -persist-cap Ar n
Pending disk writes per node.
Default 2.
.It Fl -reply-cap Ar n
Client replies recorded in the ghost log.
Default 8.
.It Fl -net Cm fifo | bag
.Cm fifo ,
the default, has one ordered channel per pair of nodes, as TCP does, and
never duplicates.
.Cm bag
is an unordered multiset that may duplicate.
.It Fl -live-steps Ar k
Bound, in steps of the fair schedule, for eventually-properties.
Default 1000.
.El
.Pp
Going past a cap is reported as an engine error, never silently
truncated.
.Ss Output
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -trace-out Ar file
On a violation, write the counterexample as a replay file.
.It Fl -progress Ar seconds
Print a progress line every
.Ar seconds
during breadth-first search; 0 turns it off.
Default 60.
.It Fl -quiet
Do not print the counterexample.
.It Fl -coverage
Print, for each action kind, the states it generated and the new
distinct states it found.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ev CMC_PRELOAD
The path of
.Pa cmc-preload.so ,
the library every checked node runs under.
By default it is found beside the running program (a build tree), then
in
.Pa ../lib/libcmc
from it (an installed
.Pa bin/cmc ) .
A program of your own that calls
.Fn cmc_main
must set it.
A node it did not load into is an error.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa *.model
Model files; see
.Lk https://gregburd.codeberg.page/libcmc/reference/model-file/ "the model file" .
.It Pa cmc-preload.so
A deterministic heap and a logical clock for the checked program.
.It Pa *.replay
Replay files.
A header names the model, settings and budgets; each following
.Ql act
line is one action in hexadecimal, with its description as a comment.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
No property was violated, and the search was complete for its mode:
breadth-first or depth-first search finished, the walks ran, or the
replay ended.
.It 1
A property was violated, an engine rule was broken (determinism,
durability, a cap), or a witness was never reached.
.It 2
Incomplete: a state, time or depth limit stopped the search before it
finished, with no violation found.
.It 3
Usage error, a bad setting, a model file that cannot be used, or a
replay file that cannot be run.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Check a server as it ships, then a build of it with a planted bug:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ cmc --model-file kvserver.model
\&...
Model checking completed. No error has been found.
$ cmc --model-file kvserver.model --binary ./kvserver-bug
Error: Invariant no_lost_ack is violated.
\&...
.Ed
.Pp
Check every property of the toy lock server, an in-process model:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ build/cmc --model toy --all-props
\&...
Model checking completed. No error has been found.
\&...
49 states generated, 34 distinct states found, 0 states left on queue.
The depth of the complete state graph search is 11.
.Ed
.Pp
Find the planted bug in
.Cm toy-bug1 ,
save the counterexample, and replay it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ build/cmc --model toy-bug1 --inv Mutex --trace-out bug1.replay
Error: Invariant Mutex is violated.
  2 clients in the critical section
\&...
$ build/cmc --replay bug1.replay --inv Mutex
.Ed
.Pp
Check a calibration configuration against its TLC result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ build/cmc --model hc \e
    --tlc-cfg calib/hc/cfg/hc_paper_cp_n3_s1.cfg --det
.Ed
.Pp
Search a large space by random walks first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ ./mycmc --model mymodel --all-props --crashes 1 --drops 1 \e
    --simulate --seed 7 --walks 100000 --depth 60
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libcmc 3 ,
.Xr cmc 7
.Pp
The guide and design notes:
.Lk https://gregburd.codeberg.page/libcmc/
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Greg Burd Aq Mt greg@burd.me
.Sh CAVEATS
A pass covers only the budgets given.
Raise
.Fl -clients ,
.Fl -drops
and
.Fl -crashes
until the bugs you planted are caught, and treat a run stopped by
.Fl -max-states
or
.Fl -time
as "no violation found in N states".
.Pp
.Fl -net Cm bag
allows behaviour a FIFO transport never shows, so a counterexample found
in bag mode may be spurious for a protocol that relies on FIFO order.
